First of all, why not use https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/moby/sys/mountinfo#Mounted directly. It is faster, optimized, maintained, and tested. It tries openat2 first, when tries the same method as `IsLikelyNotMountPoint` employs, when goes on to parse mountinfo.

If you don't want it, you can still optimize your code further:
* do call openat2 first -- this is the fastest method;
* do not call filepath.EvalSymlinks before calling openat2 -- it is not needed;
* for the above to work, you need to drop `O_NOFOLLOW` flag to openat2.

In fact when looking at it I realised that mountinfo.Mounted doc is not updated to reflect the fact it does not require normalized path. Opened https://github.com/moby/sys/pull/86 to fix.

@mkimuram thank you so much 🙏🏻  I have opened https://github.com/moby/sys/issues/88

We have some tests checking mountinfo.Mounted behavior here: https://github.com/moby/sys/blob/master/mountinfo/mounted_linux_test.go

Apparently we need to cover more cases. Not sure what to do with false positive from mountedByStat (item c in your description).

> So, the action item here is to review k8s.io/mount-utils and its users, eliminate the double read for cases in which it is not needed (potentially changing its API in the process).

If we narrow this down to only determining mount points, what can be done is:

1. figure out which users of `IsNotMountPoint` need potential re-reading of mountinfo; the criteria are:
   - a user should care about bind mounts;
   - getting a false negatives will seriously disrupt workflow;
2. if any such users are found, implement something in moby/sys/mountinfo to support re-reading (most probably a separate function).
3. merge `IsNotMountPoint` and `IsLikelyNotMountPoint` into a single function, using moby/sys/mountinfo;

I can do 2 and 3, but not really sure about 1 -- need some help with it.
